# Visual Design Standards

- Treat art direction and visual quality as core product requirements, not final polish.
- Before designing, inspect the available assets and existing design system. Use supplied assets as the primary visual reference.
- Never invent asset filenames or replace supplied media with stock images without a clear reason.
- Aim for a distinctive, coherent creative direction rather than a generic SaaS template.
- Prioritize typography, hierarchy, composition, negative space, contrast, rhythm, and intentional image crops.
- Establish reusable design tokens for colors, typography, spacing, radii, and motion where appropriate.
- Avoid arbitrary gradients, excessive glassmorphism, repetitive card grids, unnecessary borders, and decorative effects without purpose.
- Design the hero and first viewport deliberately before expanding into secondary sections.
- Make desktop, tablet, and mobile layouts intentional. Do not merely shrink the desktop design.
- Use responsive images, explicit dimensions or aspect ratios, and appropriate loading strategies to reduce layout shift.
- Preserve semantic HTML, keyboard navigation, readable contrast, and visible focus states.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion and provide usable fallbacks for motion-heavy experiences.
- Validate the actual browser rendering when browser tools are available; do not claim visual verification without inspecting it.
- When a visual change is requested, explain the intended effect briefly and implement it without unrelated redesigns.